---
interface Props {
	name:
		| 'email' | 'linkedin' | 'github' | 'twitter'
		| 'drums' | 'invader' | 'clapper' | 'boot' | 'wave'
		| 'astro' | 'javascript' | 'tailwind' | 'react' | 'figma' | 'claude' | 'git';
}
const { name } = Astro.props;
---

{
	name === 'email' && (
		<svg class="icon-email" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			<rect style="--len: 70.9" x="2" y="4" width="20" height="16" rx="0.5" />
			{/* Butt caps: the flap's ends sit at the frame stroke's centerline, so
			    square caps would poke visibly through the envelope's silhouette. */}
			<path style="--len: 23.4" stroke-linecap="butt" d="m22 7-10 6L2 7" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{/* The three brand marks are redrawn as 2px square-cap strokes — the same
    drawing system as the email icon and the theme toggle's contrast glyph —
    so the whole nav row reads as one hand rather than mixing outline and
    solid-fill densities. GitHub and Twitter keep their silhouettes; LinkedIn
    trades its solid slab for an "in" box.

    stroke-miterlimit="2" (on every icon) bevels the one pathological spike:
    Twitter's `z` closepath rejoins at a razor angle, and the default miter
    limit of 4 lets that join shoot a needle well past the top-right corner.
    A 90° corner's miter ratio is 1.41, so email/linkedin/github are untouched.

    GitHub's ears and Twitter's wings otherwise run right to the viewBox edge,
    where `overflow: hidden` clips them flat and they read as cut off when
    zoomed. Each is inset with a centered group transform, and the group's
    stroke-width is 2/scale so the drawn weight stays exactly 2 viewBox units
    after scaling. (NOT vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke": that pins the
    stroke to 2 *screen* px, which at the nav's ~17px render size is far
    heavier than the others' scaled ~1.4px.)
    Measured painted bounds land all four icons ≥0.6 units clear of the box.

    --len on every shape is its measured getTotalLength() (rounded UP a hair),
    which the load intro's stroke-draw keyframes use for true-unit dash math
    (see the load-intro block in global.css). NOT pathLength normalization:
    Chrome ignores pathLength when computing dash rendering, so a normalized
    pattern runs in true units anyway — short strokes popped in instantly and
    long ones finished early. Rounding up (never down) matters: the final
    dash must cover the whole path, and on closed shapes the overshoot wraps
    the seam so it welds shut. Re-measure --len if a d= ever changes. */}
{
	name === 'linkedin' && (
		<svg class="icon-linkedin" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			<rect style="--len: 70.9" x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="0.5" />
			<path style="--len: 5.05" d="M8 11v5" />
			{/* The i-dot is a REAL 2.01-unit upstroke, not a point: with butt caps
			    a length-2.01 vertical path paints the exact 2×2 square the old
			    square-capped 0.01-unit point did (caps added 1 unit each way), but
			    now the load intro can stroke-draw it — bottom-up, since the dash
			    reveals from the path start, so the dot strokes UPWARD in step with
			    the stem beneath it. */}
			<path style="--len: 2.05" stroke-linecap="butt" d="M8 8.81V6.8" />
			<path style="--len: 5.05" d="M12 16v-5" />
			<path style="--len: 10.2" d="M12 13.4a2.4 2.4 0 0 1 4.8 0V16" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'github' && (
		<svg class="icon-github" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			<g transform="translate(0.65 0.77) scale(0.98)" stroke-width="2.041">
				{/* The arm is its own butt-capped path: its start is buried inside
				    the left leg's stroke (a square cap there pokes out of the leg),
				    and its tail is extended ~1u along the end tangent so the butt
				    end lands where the square cap's tip used to. */}
				<path style="--len: 9.25" stroke-linecap="butt" d="M9 19c-5 1.5-5-2.5-7.97-3.24" />
				<path style="--len: 66.6" d="M16 22v-3.87a3.37 3.37 0 0 0-.94-2.61c3.14-.35 6.44-1.54 6.44-7A5.44 5.44 0 0 0 20 4.77 5.07 5.07 0 0 0 19.91 1S18.73.65 16 2.48a13.38 13.38 0 0 0-7 0C6.27.65 5.09 1 5.09 1A5.07 5.07 0 0 0 5 4.77a5.44 5.44 0 0 0-1.5 3.78c0 5.42 3.3 6.61 6.44 7A3.37 3.37 0 0 0 9 18.13V22" />
			</g>
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'twitter' && (
		<svg class="icon-twitter" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			<g transform="translate(1.18 0.9) scale(0.93)" stroke-width="2.151">
				<path style="--len: 84.1" d="M23 3a10.9 10.9 0 0 1-3.14 1.53 4.48 4.48 0 0 0-7.86 3v1A10.66 10.66 0 0 1 3 4s-4 9 5 13a11.64 11.64 0 0 1-8 2c9 5 20 0 20-11.5a4.5 4.5 0 0 0-.08-.83A7.72 7.72 0 0 0 23 3z" />
			</g>
		</svg>
	)
}

{/* ── The about page's marks (2026-08-04) ──
    These five replaced 👋🥁👾🎬🥾, which were the only emoji on the site and the
    only marks on it that no token could reach: OS colour bitmaps take no
    --ink-*, can't mirror per theme (🎬's black body sank into the night
    ground), render as a different typeface per platform — so what you check in
    `astro dev` isn't what a visitor gets — and, being saturated colour against
    a 300/400/500 weight scale, out-weighted the very labels they were marking.

    EACH ONE REDRAWS ITS OWN EMOJI and is not free to become a neighbouring
    idea (Hiro, 08-04, after a first pass wandered): 👾 is an invader, not a
    gamepad; 🎬 is a clapperboard, not a film strip; 🥾 is a boot, not a
    mountain. The label beside each already names the interest, so an icon
    that generalises it says strictly less than the emoji it replaced.

    They carry a colour each — see the --mark-* tokens in global.css, where
    the values and their ratios live.

    NO --len on any shape, deliberately. The load intro's stroke-draw is scoped
    to the theme toggle's circle alone (see the load-intro block in global.css),
    so nothing here is ever dashed and there is no length to keep in sync. If
    one of these ever moves into the nav, measure getTotalLength() first — the
    four above document why.

    2026-08-15: the four contact marks above are drawn by the intro at 640px
    and up ONLY. Below that the bar shows the contact menu instead of the icon
    row (see the contact-surface block in global.css), the row is display: none,
    and the intro wipes the menu's trigger in its place. Their --len values
    matter at every width regardless — the same marks are used inside the menu's
    rows, one media query away from being stroked again.

    Same drawing system as the contact icons otherwise: 24-unit box, 2px
    stroke, square caps, miter joins at limit 2. Butt caps appear only on
    interior detail whose ends are meant to die into a surrounding shape —
    the same exception the email flap takes, and here it is load-bearing
    rather than cosmetic: a square cap on the snare's zigzag overhangs a full
    unit along its own diagonal and lands inside the drum's left wall. */}
{
	name === 'drums' && (
		<svg class="icon-drums" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			<ellipse cx="12" cy="6.5" rx="9" ry="3" />
			<path d="M3 6.5v10a9 3 0 0 0 18 0v-10" />
			{/* Tension rods. Peaks held at y=12 rather than tucked up under the
			    rim: the head's lower stroke reaches y=10.5 at centre, and a peak
			    any higher welds the zigzag onto the ellipse at exactly the point
			    where the two are meant to read as separate parts. */}
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M5.4 12 8.7 16.2 12 12l3.3 4.2L18.6 12" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'invader' && (
		<svg class="icon-invader" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* One closed silhouette on a 2-unit grid, every segment orthogonal.
			    That's the whole reason this one works in the site's hand: square
			    caps and miter joins ARE the pixel-art idiom, so a stepped outline
			    reads as sprite rather than as an icon of a sprite. Nothing here
			    may go diagonal — a single slope and it stops being pixels.

			    Shoulders step OUT (x3/x21) past the head (x6/x18) and the three
			    feet step down: those two moves are what separate an invader from
			    a rounded blob, and they survive being drawn at 20px where the
			    eyes almost don't. */}
			<path d="M6 7h12v3h3v4h-3v3h-2v-2h-2v2h-4v-2h-2v2H6v-3H3v-4h3Z" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M9 7V4.5" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M15 7V4.5" />
			{/* 2×2 eyes, the LinkedIn i-dot trick again: a butt-capped 2-unit
			    vertical at stroke-width 2 paints the exact square. */}
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M10 10.5v2" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M14 10.5v2" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'clapper' && (
		<svg class="icon-clapper" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			<rect x="2" y="10" width="20" height="10" rx="0.5" />
			{/* The bar is hinged at the left and OPEN — its underside climbs from
			    y9.9 to y7.3 while the slate's top edge stays at 10, and the
			    wedge of ground that opens up between them is the only thing
			    saying "clapperboard" rather than "box with a lid". Close the
			    angle and it stops reading.

			    NO diagonal stripes. The bar is 3.2 units thick, of which 2 are
			    its own stroke, so a stripe has 1.2 units of clear interior to
			    live in — at this size it fills the bar solid rather than
			    striping it. Tried at 4.2 thick too; the bar then out-weighs the
			    slate it sits on. The tilt carries the read on its own. */}
			<path d="M2 9.9 21.6 7.3V4.1L2 6.7Z" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'boot' && (
		<svg class="icon-boot" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* The TOE IS AN ARC and the shaft is wide — both were the fix. The
			    first draft ran the toe out as a straight diagonal (`v7 l6 2`) off
			    a narrow shaft, and at 20px it read as a bracket or a chair, not
			    footwear: a boot's silhouette is a tall back and a round front,
			    and a straight wedge gives it neither.

			    The sole is a band the full width of the silhouette that
			    overhangs at the HEEL (x4.5 against the upper's 6). Overhang is
			    the hiking part — a sole flush with the upper draws a dress
			    boot — and it goes at the back because the toe already has the
			    arc to carry its own weight. */}
			<path d="M6 4h7v7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5v2H4.5v-2H6Z" />
			{/* Two lace bars, butt-capped so their ends die inside the shaft's
			    walls rather than poking through them. */}
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M7.6 6.5h3.8" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M7.6 9h3.8" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'wave' && (
		<svg class="icon-wave" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* The one icon on the site that is mostly ARCS, and it has to be:
			    fingers are capsules, and stepping them the way the invader is
			    stepped would draw a comb. It keeps the 2px weight and the miter
			    limit, which is what holds it in the same hand.

			    Every path is butt-capped, without exception. Three of the four
			    are finger backs whose lower ends are meant to disappear into the
			    palm; a square cap there adds a unit of stroke that surfaces
			    through the palm's own outline as a stray notch. */}
			{/* Tilted 18° (Hiro, 08-05). Upright, an open hand is the STOP gesture —
			    the same drawing means halt at 0° and hello off-vertical, and no
			    amount of redrawing the fingers fixes that. Rotated about (12,13)
			    rather than the box centre: the pivot sits in the palm, so the
			    wrist stays put and the fingers swing, which is where a real wave
			    turns. Painted bounds still clear the box — the hand's widest
			    reach is ~19.5 units and 18° of rotation costs it under 2. */}
			<g transform="rotate(18 12 13)">
				<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M18 11V6a2 2 0 0 0-4 0" />
				<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M14 10V4a2 2 0 0 0-4 0v2" />
				<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M10 10.5V6a2 2 0 0 0-4 0v8" />
				<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M18 8a2 2 0 1 1 4 0v6a8 8 0 0 1-8 8h-2c-2.8 0-4.5-.86-5.99-2.34l-3.6-3.6a2 2 0 0 1 2.83-2.82L7 15" />
			</g>
		</svg>
	)
}

{/* ── The toolbox marks (2026-08-05) ──
    Seven tools, and unlike the interests marks these are BRAND marks.

    Brand marks are drawn as FILLS by their owners and this site draws
    strokes, so an outline trace of a solid logo comes back as a doubled
    contour. Each of these is redrawn as a single-stroke skeleton instead —
    the same trade the nav made when LinkedIn's solid slab became an "in"
    box. They are recognisable, not accurate; anything needing accuracy
    should ship the vendor's own SVG and sit outside this file.

    One is deliberately GENERIC: Claude Code is a terminal, because
    Anthropic's mark is a burst of TAPERED rays and taper is the one thing a
    uniform 2px stroke cannot do — traced, it comes out an asterisk. A bad
    trace of a famous mark reads as a mistake; an honest generic reads as a
    category.

    Colour lives in the --tool-* tokens in global.css, one hue per tool, with
    the brand each is answering to and the note that green is unavailable.

    TWO MARKS WERE HERE AND ARE GONE — `markup` (an HTML/CSS code caret) and
    `aws` (a cloud), removed with their list entries the same day. Keeping
    why, because both would be re-drawn the same way by anyone adding them
    back: HTML and CSS ship the same shield in two colours, so in a row that
    reads by hue they were one mark repeated; and AWS's smile-arrow
    identifies nothing without the wordmark above it, which left a generic
    cloud standing for a specific claim. Neither drawing was the problem.
    The list entries were, and the note on `tools` in about.astro has that. */}
{
	name === 'astro' && (
		<svg class="icon-astro" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* The mark's silhouette reduced to its A: two outer legs and the
			    inner apex, open at the foot the way the original is. */}
			<path d="M12 2.5 4.5 20h4l3.5-10.5L15.5 20h4Z" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'javascript' && (
		<svg class="icon-javascript" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* The tile with a J and an S set as single strokes. The only
			    lettering anywhere in this file, and near its floor at 20px — if
			    the toolbox ever renders smaller than the interests list, this is
			    the first mark that stops working. */}
			<rect x="2.5" y="2.5" width="19" height="19" rx="0.5" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M11 8.6v6.1a2.2 2.2 0 0 1-4.4 0" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M18.2 9.5a2.4 2.4 0 0 0-4 1.4c0 2.1 4 1.6 4 3.6a2.4 2.4 0 0 1-4 1.3" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'tailwind' && (
		<svg class="icon-tailwind" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* Two waves, offset right and down — the brand's own arrangement,
			    drawn as centrelines rather than as the filled ribbon it really
			    is. Stroking the ribbon's outline gives four wavy lines where the
			    mark has two. */}
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M2 11c1.8-3.7 4.4-3.7 6.2 0s4.4 3.7 6.2 0" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M7.6 16.4c1.8-3.7 4.4-3.7 6.2 0s4.4 3.7 6.2 0" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'react' && (
		<svg class="icon-react" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* The one brand mark here that IS a line drawing already, so it needs
			    no reinterpretation. rx 9.4 keeps the painted ellipse inside the
			    box once the 2px stroke is added. */}
			<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2" />
			<ellipse cx="12" cy="12" rx="9.4" ry="3.6" />
			<ellipse cx="12" cy="12" rx="9.4" ry="3.6" transform="rotate(60 12 12)" />
			<ellipse cx="12" cy="12" rx="9.4" ry="3.6" transform="rotate(120 12 12)" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'figma' && (
		<svg class="icon-figma" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* Five parts on a 2×3 grid, the arrangement being the whole mark —
			    two capsules top, a capsule and a circle mid, a capsule bottom
			    left. Rounded on each cell's outer edge and square where a cell
			    meets its neighbour, which is what makes the stack read as one
			    object rather than five. */}
			<path d="M12 3.2H9a2.9 2.9 0 0 0 0 5.8h3Z" />
			<path d="M12 3.2h3a2.9 2.9 0 0 1 0 5.8h-3Z" />
			<path d="M12 9H9a2.9 2.9 0 0 0 0 5.8h3Z" />
			<circle cx="15" cy="11.9" r="2.9" />
			<path d="M12 14.8H9a2.9 2.9 0 1 0 3 2.9Z" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'claude' && (
		<svg class="icon-claude" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* GENERIC, and the honest choice: Anthropic's mark is a radial burst
			    of TAPERED rays, and taper is exactly what a uniform 2px stroke
			    cannot do — traced here it comes out an asterisk. This is a
			    terminal, which is what Claude Code actually is. */}
			<rect x="2" y="4" width="20" height="16" rx="0.5" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="m7.2 10 3 2.6-3 2.6" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M12.6 15.2h4.4" />
		</svg>
	)
}
{
	name === 'git' && (
		<svg class="icon-git" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="square" stroke-linejoin="miter" stroke-miterlimit="2">
			{/* The branch, not the lozenge. Git's own mark is a filled rotated
			    square with three nodes and reads as a logo nobody recognises out
			    of context; branch-and-merge is what people picture when they
			    hear the word. */}
			<circle cx="6.5" cy="5.5" r="2.6" />
			<circle cx="6.5" cy="18.5" r="2.6" />
			<circle cx="17.5" cy="5.5" r="2.6" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M6.5 8.1v7.8" />
			<path stroke-linecap="butt" d="M17.5 8.1v1.4a4 4 0 0 1-4 4h-3a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v.5" />
		</svg>
	)
}
